WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.560
Most sensory receptors are excited by their stimulus.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.240
Rods and cones are quieted by theirs.

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.980
A rod sitting in the dark is depolarized.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:16.220
Current flows steadily into it, and it releases glutamate all the time.

5
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:17.980
Light turns it down.

6
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.120
How does quieting a cell let you see?

7
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:25.810
In darkness, cGMP holds CNG channels open in the outer segment.

8
00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:27.970
Sodium and calcium flow in.

9
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.150
That steady dark current keeps the rod depolarized, so it releases glutamate.

10
00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.030
Now a photon is absorbed by rhodopsin.

11
00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.370
One activated rhodopsin activates many transducins.

12
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.810
Transducin activates PDE6, which breaks down cGMP.

13
00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.190
The channels close, and the dark current shrinks.

14
00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.730
The rod hyperpolarizes, and glutamate release falls.

15
00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.143
The cone releases one transmitter, glutamate.

16
00:00:53.823 --> 00:00:55.863
It releases more in the dark and less in the light.

17
00:00:56.343 --> 00:01:00.003
Yet some bipolar cells depolarize to light, and others hyperpolarize.

18
00:01:00.703 --> 00:01:03.723
Glutamate's sign is set by the receptor, not by the molecule.

19
00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:07.643
OFF bipolar cells use AMPA or kainate receptors.

20
00:01:08.283 --> 00:01:12.243
In the dark, glutamate opens cation channels and depolarizes them.

21
00:01:13.003 --> 00:01:15.843
Light removes that excitation, so they hyperpolarize.

22
00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:17.363
The sign is preserved.

23
00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:22.687
ON bipolar cells use a different receptor, mGluR6.

24
00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:27.847
In the dark, glutamate on mGluR6 activates the G protein Go.

25
00:01:28.407 --> 00:01:31.247
Go keeps the channel TRPM1 closed.

26
00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:32.887
Light lowers glutamate.

27
00:01:33.547 --> 00:01:36.547
Go activity declines, and TRPM1 opens.

28
00:01:37.187 --> 00:01:38.287
The cell depolarizes.

29
00:01:38.847 --> 00:01:39.867
The sign is inverted.

30
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.630
So each cone feeds two parallel streams, one signalling lighter and one signalling

31
00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.270
darker. ON bipolar cells drive ON ganglion cells.

32
00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.270
OFF bipolar cells drive OFF ganglion cells.

33
00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:57.810
In cat retina, light on the centre of a field makes an ON centre ganglion cell fire

34
00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.430
faster, and suppresses an OFF centre cell.

35
00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.630
Ganglion cells fire action potentials.

36
00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.530
They are the retina's only output.

37
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:11.070
Light quiets a cone, and the retina reads that drop in two opposite ways.

38
00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:13.410
OFF bipolar cells keep the sign.

39
00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.750
ON bipolar cells invert it.

40
00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.710
Keep this rule. The receptor, not the molecule, sets the sign.
